Part of Cloudberry's greenplum_schedule on the port's cluster: the
differences from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and
are the port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test as
passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds no
difference or exactly the file's.

correlated_subquery, qp_derived_table, qp_misc (orca)  the lines ORCA's
  fallback trace prints and nothing else: each test sets
  optimizer_trace_fallback, and ORCA declines the plans Cloudberry makes with
  a Gather Motion into a slice the segments run -- one segment gathering
  another's rows, which the port's Motions do not do yet -- so PostgreSQL's
  planner plans them, with the same answers, and the trace says so.

as_alias  an ORDER BY of text, which puts "XXXX" before "bbbb": the cluster
  is made with the C locale, and Cloudberry's expected output with one that
  sorts letters before case.

bfv_dd_multicolumn (planner, orca)  the INFO lines of
  test_print_direct_dispatch_info.  Cloudberry sends a query whose key is
  fixed to a few values to those segments ("PARTIAL contents: 1 2"), where
  the port sends it to one segment or every one; the lines of its two-phase
  commit ("Distributed transaction command ...") are M3's; its INSERT ...
  SELECT of generate_series() runs the function in a slice of one segment,
  and its ANALYZE dispatches a slice, where the port's run the one on the
  coordinator and sample through O3.  Under ORCA, an INSERT of one row of
  constants into a table whose key has two columns goes to every segment:
  ORCA's core gives direct dispatch to a single-column key only.

case_gp  IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, and the CASE form, printed as the searched
  CASE the port's rewrite makes of them (see the singlenode suite's README).

qp_union_intersect  an error from a segment names the segment in its
  DETAIL; and an UPDATE of the distribution key that finds one row twice
  moves it once, as PostgreSQL's UPDATE ... FROM changes it once, where
  Cloudberry's Split refuses it ("multiple updates to a row by the same
  query is not allowed").

with_clause  three recursive queries Cloudberry refuses -- a join of nested
  RECURSIVE clauses, and window functions in a recursive query's target
  list -- which PostgreSQL 19 answers.
